{"records":[{"id":"t6g8bfpgvk","title":"M. Jost et al,. Mol Cell 2017 - Figure 5D - Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent","description":"Microscopy files used for the analysis of data presented in figure 5D of\r\nM. Jost et al., Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent. 2017","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-08-29T10:28:07.227Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t6g8bfpgvk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t6g8bfpgvk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lenno","last_name":"Krenning","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ankit","last_name":"Rai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Akhmanova","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","In Vitro Techniques"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"dfdxy4ksh3","title":"Figure 4 - Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent","description":"Microscopy files used for the analysis of data presented in figures 4 and S4 of\r\nM. Jost et al., Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent. 2017","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-07-18T06:50:43.254Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dfdxy4ksh3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dfdxy4ksh3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lenno","last_name":"Krenning","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marvin","last_name":"Tanenbaum","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Microscopy"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"tm22dtht9x","title":"M. Jost et al,. Mol Cell 2017 - Figure 5A - Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent","description":"Microscopy files used for the analysis of data presented in figure 5A of\r\nM. Jost et al., Combined CRISPRi/a-based chemical genetic screens reveal that rigosertib is a microtubule-destabilizing agent. Mol. Cell. 2017","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-08-29T10:27:52.938Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tm22dtht9x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tm22dtht9x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lenno","last_name":"Krenning","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","In Vitro Techniques"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"6bkjdg5g85","title":"Zmat3 is a key splicing regulator in the p53 tumor suppression program","description":"Although TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancers, the p53-dependent transcriptional programs mediating tumor suppression remain incompletely understood. Here, to uncover critical components downstream of p53 in tumor suppression, we perform unbiased RNAi and CRISPR/Cas9-based genetic screens in vivo.  These screens converge upon the p53-inducible gene Zmat3, encoding an RNA-binding-protein, and we demonstrate that ZMAT3 is an important tumor suppressor downstream of p53 in mouse KrasG12D-driven lung and liver cancers and human carcinomas. Integrative analysis of the ZMAT3 RNA-binding landscape and transcriptomic profiling reveals that ZMAT3 directly modulates exon inclusion in transcripts encoding proteins of diverse functions, including the p53 inhibitors MDM4 and MDM2, splicing regulators, and components of varied cellular processes. Interestingly, these exons are enriched in NMD signals, and, accordingly, ZMAT3 broadly affects target transcript stability. Collectively, these studies reveal ZMAT3 as a novel RNA-splicing and homeostasis regulator and key component of p53-mediated tumor suppression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T16:30:19.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6bkjdg5g85","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6bkjdg5g85"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kathryn","last_name":"Bieging-Rolett","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Tumor","Alternative Splicing","RNA Sequencing","P53","Mouse Model of Cancer","Adenocarcinoma","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tdngvdyx35","title":"Data of role of Sodium tetraborate in the Modulation of Cardiac Hypertrophic ","description":"Using, a hypertrophy model was established. Sodium tetraborate is a cardioprotective and / or competitive agent that modulates genetic reprogramming in neonatal cardiomyocytes treated with para isoproterenol as a hypertrophic stimulus.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-08T17:40:01.526Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tdngvdyx35","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tdngvdyx35"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ALEJANDRA","last_name":"CONTRERAS-RAMOS","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Acquisition","Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9w4g54bv43","title":"Genome-wide screens reveal that resveratrol induces replicative stress in human cells","description":"Resveratrol is a natural product associated with wide-ranging effects in animal and cellular models including lifespan extension. To identify the genetic target of resveratrol in human cells, we conducted genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screens to pinpoint genes that confer sensitivity or resistance to resveratrol. An extensive network of DNA damage response and replicative stress genes exhibited genetic interactions with resveratrol and its analog pterostilbene. These genetic profiles showed similarity to the response to hydroxyurea, an inhibitor of ribonucleotide reductase that causes replicative stress. Resveratrol, pterostilbene and hydroxyurea caused similar depletion of nucleotide pools, inhibition of replication fork progression and induction of replicative stress. The ability of resveratrol to inhibit cell proliferation and S phase transit was independent of the histone deacetylase Sirtuin 1, which has been implicated in lifespan extension by resveratrol. These results establish that a primary impact of resveratrol on human cell proliferation is the induction of low-level replicative stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-04T14:56:19.488Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9w4g54bv43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9w4g54bv43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahya","last_name":"Benslimane","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thierry","last_name":"Bertomeu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mary","last_name":"McQuaid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Sánchez-Osuna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Papadopoli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daina","last_name":"Avizonis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariana","last_name":"De Sa Tavares Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Huard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Topisirovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hugo","last_name":"Wurtele","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mike","last_name":"Tyers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lea","last_name":"Harrington","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","High-Throughput Screening","High-Throughput Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7g9dvjvswg","title":"Cytoskeleton dynamics during lateral root initiation in Arabidopsis thaliana","description":"Morphogenesis of lateral roots relies on the asymmetric cell division of initially symmetric founder cells. This division is preceded by the tightly controlled asymmetric radial expansion of these cells. The cellular mechanisms that license and ensure the coordination of these events are unknown. Here, we quantitatively analyse microtubule and F-actin dynamics during lateral root initiation. Using mutants, pharmacological and tissue-specific genetic perturbations, we show that dynamic reorganisation of both microtubule and F-actin networks is necessary for the asymmetric expansion of the founder cells. This cytoskeleton remodelling intertwines with auxin signalling in the pericycle and endodermis in order for founder cells to acquire a basic polarity required for initiating lateral root development.  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-06-11T14:31:35.673Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7g9dvjvswg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7g9dvjvswg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Maizel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Plant Biology","Microscopy","Cytoskeleton","Root"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zdnrtmyfgh","title":"A Chemical-Genetic Interaction Matrix Reveals Drug Mechanism and Genetic Architecture","description":"This dataset is associated with an article bearing the same name ( https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.01.28.701823 ), describing the results of >400 CRISPR/Cas9 chemical-genetic genome-wide knockout screens covering 305 different compounds and 5 conditions. The dataset also contains meta-data about each compound as well as the raw and/or processed data necessary to reproduce various analyses and figures from the article. The contents of each table and sub-table (tab) are described in the overview tab of each file. Table contents are briefly described below.\nTable S1. Summary of the CGI dataset. (A) Sequenced samples. (B) Independent screens. (C) Compound details and verification. (D) NALM-6 gene expression. (E) CGIs detected.\nTable S2. CRANKS scores.\nTable S3. Known compound-target relationships.\nTable S4. Combined CRANKS scores for two independent TRAIL screens.\nTable S5. Jaccard similarity indices between screens in the CGI dataset. Pairs used in Fig. 2D are indicated by class.\nTable S6. Compound-transporter CGI scores.\nTable S7. (A) Compound dose-to-solubility ratios by screen. (B) Correlation coefficients of CRANKS scores with compound dose-to-solubility ratios. \nTable S8. Vemurafenib SSL hits and Gene Ontology annotations.\nTable S9. Predicted basic pKa and logP for compounds represented in the screen similarity network.\nTable S10. Mean genome-wide CRANKS score profile of the 28 screens in the phospholipidosis cluster.\nTable S11. LipidTOX Red assay determinations. (A) Cell counts and average LipidTOX levels. (B) Compound classification.\nTable S12. Average CRANKS scores for combined screens. (A) Nocodazole. (B) Active site mTOR inhibitors. (C) Rapamycin. (D) AICAR. (E) Pyridostatin and topoisomerase II inhibitors. (F) DHFR inhibitor screens. (G) PARP inhibitors. (H) CHEK1 inhibitors.\nTable S13. Comparison of CGIs for 9 shared compounds in this dataset and the Olivieri et al (2020) dataset. \nTable S14. Data for assessment of compound synergy/antagonism, including luminescence readings, treatments by well and log2 Bliss scores determined by compound pair.\nTable S15. (A) CRANKS2 scores. (B) Top 20,000 most correlated gene pairs.\nTable S16. RNA-seq data. (A) FK-506 versus DMSO and read counts by gene. (B) PPP3CA knockout versus wild-type controls. (C) PPP3R1 knockout versus wild-type controls. (D) Transcription rates in PABIR1 knockout clones.\nTable S17. List of DDR-associated genes.\nTable S18. (A) Significant exon-level CGIs. (B) Exon classification and validation data.\nTable S19. (A) Hypothetical gene CGIs with p-values <0.002 or absolute scores ≥2.5. (B) Hypothetical gene classification and mass spectral counts.\nTable S20. Correlation between gene CRANKS scores and population doublings.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-02-09T12:56:30.064Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zdnrtmyfgh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zdnrtmyfgh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":"FDN-167277, FDN–167265, PJT190250, PJT152920, PJT-451236, 163051, 201311MFE"},{"id":"https://ror.org/000az4664","name":"Canada Foundation for Innovation","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h531d29","name":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada","grant_id":"RGPIN-2022-04206 Canada Research Chairs, https://ror.org/0517h6h17"},{"id":"https://ror.org/05vd00053","name":"Terry Fox Foundation","grant_id":"https://ror.org/05vd00053, TFF-242122"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01 OD010929, R35 GM127032, RO1 NCI80728, RO1 NCI98571"},{"id":"https://ror.org/000dswa46","name":"W. M. Keck Foundation","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"IVADO","grant_id":"PD-2018b-AP-05"},{"id":null,"name":"McLaughlin Centre Accelerator","grant_id":"MC-2023-07, MC-2024-08-3"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043q8yx54","name":"Ontario Institute for Cancer Research","grant_id":"https://ror.org/043q8yx54, SU2C-AACR-DT-19–15"},{"id":null,"name":"Institute for Research in Immunology and Cancer","grant_id":"https://ror.org/00wj6x496, SPC1901, SPC2102, SPC2104, La Fondation Famille Godin"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04m0xav37","name":"Leukemia and Lymphoma Society","grant_id":"MC_UU_00035/13"},{"id":"https://ror.org/029s29983","name":"Genome Canada","grant_id":"LSARP4524, LSARP13528, SU2C-AACR-DT-19–15, Centre for Advanced Proteomic and Chemogenomic Analyses"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00xhtr962","name":"Génome Québec","grant_id":"https://ror.org/00xhtr962, LSARP4524, Centre for Advanced Proteomic and Chemogenomic Analyses"},{"id":"https://ror.org/02fnpv153","name":"American Association For Cancer Research","grant_id":"https://ror.org/02fnpv153, SU2C-AACR-DT-19–15"}],"categories":["Genetics","Bioinformatics","Systems Biology","Pharmacogenomics","Gene Knockout","Computational Biology","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fzf8z554dp","title":"WHO SAYS WHAT TO WHOM?","description":"When using discourse, people reveal what they did or what another person or entity did to someone else or about something; that is, in discourse an agent and someone (something) emerge who receives the action of the agent or recipient. This proposal establishes an analysis model to identify the Agent and the Recipient.\nKey Words\nDiscourse analysis. Agent. Recipient","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-18T18:37:57.495Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fzf8z554dp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fzf8z554dp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pablo","last_name":"Ojeda Lopeda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yamileth","last_name":"Bejarano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Beatriz","last_name":"Rivera","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04td15k45","name":"Universidad Cooperativa de Colombia","grant_id":"Acta de inicio INV3168"}],"categories":["Psychology","Psycholinguistics"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":96705,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":130,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":96705,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":96425,"restricted":52,"embargoed":228},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1553},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":471},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":373},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":336},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":329},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78